Description: Restore windows to their places with the session save/restore API of GTK.
 Text Editor restores its windows with their documents, sizes and maximized state from its own session state,
 but a Wayland client can neither learn nor set where its windows are, so they open wherever GNOME Shell puts
 new windows. Only the compositor can place them again, by the name the application gives each window through
 the session management protocol. GTK names the windows of an application which sets
 GtkApplication:support-save, saves the names with the rest of its window state, and at the next start emits
 GtkApplication::restore-window for each saved window.
 .
 Text Editor now sets support-save and gives every window an ID, which it saves both in the window state GTK
 keeps (GtkApplicationWindow::save-state) and with the window in its own session state. For each window GTK
 restores, an empty window is created while GTK emits restore-window, as GTK requires, and kept hidden. The
 session restore then gives it the pages of the window saved under the same ID and shows it, where mutter
 places it again. A window the session restore has no pages for is destroyed once the session has another
 window, and a window saved without a known ID is created as before, keeping its ID so it is placed from the
 next start. A standalone instance does not set support-save.
 .
 The save/restore API is public only in a GTK with patches/gtk4/, so the property and the signals are looked
 up at runtime: Text Editor builds against Ubuntu's GTK and behaves as before when it runs with it.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-11-04
